\lessonlabel{1}
\section{Introduction to Management}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define the term \term{management};
  \item list the four basic functions of management;
  \item explain why management is needed in organisations;
  \item identify the levels of management in a typical company.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{What is management?}

\term{Management} is the process of planning, organising, leading and controlling resources to achieve organisational goals efficiently and effectively. It involves making decisions, coordinating activities and motivating people.

\begin{example}
A small café owner decides the menu (planning), purchases coffee beans and hires staff (organising), motivates the barista to serve quickly (leading), and checks daily sales to adjust staff schedules (controlling).
\end{example}

\subsection{The four functions of management}

The classic model divides management into four interrelated functions:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \term{Planning} – setting objectives and deciding how to achieve them.
  \item \term{Organising} – arranging tasks, people and resources.
  \item \term{Leading} – influencing and directing people to work towards goals.
  \item \term{Controlling} – monitoring performance and correcting deviations.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
A university department plans to increase enrolment (planning), creates new course sections and assigns lecturers (organising), holds faculty meetings to inspire teaching excellence (leading), and reviews enrolment numbers each semester to adjust marketing (controlling).
\end{example}

\subsection{Why management is needed}

Without management, resources may be wasted, goals remain unclear and coordination breaks down. Management provides:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Goal alignment.} All parts of the organisation work toward the same objectives.
  \item \textbf{Resource efficiency.} People, money and equipment are used where they add most value.
  \item \textbf{Adaptability.} The organisation can respond to changes in the environment.
  \item \textbf{Motivation.} Employees receive direction and support, improving performance.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A retail chain that does not plan inventory may overstock some stores and run out of stock in others, leading to lost sales and higher costs.
\end{example}

\subsection{Levels of management}

Organisations typically have three levels:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \term{Top management} – sets overall strategy (e.g., CEO, board).
  \item \term{Middle management} – translates strategy into departmental plans (e.g., department heads).
  \item \term{First-line management} – supervises daily operations and staff (e.g., shift supervisors).
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
In a hospital, the chief medical officer (top) decides on service expansion, the department chair (middle) creates staffing schedules, and the ward manager (first-line) oversees nurses on each shift.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Identify a real organisation you know. List one activity for each of the four management functions and name a person at each management level who would be responsible for that activity.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Write a short definition of \term{management} in your own words.
  \item Match each of the four functions to a real‑world example from a school, business or club.
  \item Explain how the lack of controlling could affect a project’s budget.
  \item Identify the three levels of management in a local government office and give one responsibility for each level.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Management & Planning, organising, leading and controlling resources to achieve goals. \\
Planning & Setting objectives and deciding the actions to achieve them. \\
Organising & Arranging tasks, people and resources to implement plans. \\
Leading & Influencing and motivating people to work toward objectives. \\
Controlling & Monitoring performance and making corrections as needed. \\
Top management & Senior executives who set overall strategy. \\
Middle management & Managers who translate strategy into departmental plans. \\
First-line management & Supervisors who oversee daily operations and staff. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{2}
\section{Evolution of Management Thought}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item outline the major schools of management thought from the classical to the contemporary period;
  \item describe the key ideas and contributors of each school;
  \item explain how historical context influenced each management approach;
  \item compare the strengths and limitations of the different schools.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Classical management theory}

\term{Scientific management} was introduced by Frederick Taylor in the early 1900s. Taylor argued that work could be studied scientifically to find the “one best way” to perform a task. He emphasized time studies, standardised tools, and piece‑rate pay.

\term{Administrative management} was developed by Henri Fayol. Fayol identified five functions of management (planning, organising, commanding, coordinating, controlling) and fourteen principles such as unity of command and scalar chain.

\term{Bureaucratic management} was described by Max Weber. Weber stressed a formal hierarchy, clear rules, and impersonal relationships to achieve efficiency and predictability.

\begin{example}
A factory implements Taylor’s time study on a sewing operation. Workers are given a stopwatch and instructed to follow a prescribed sequence of motions. The manager records the fastest time and sets a piece‑rate wage based on that standard.
\end{example}

\subsection{Human relations movement}

The human relations school emerged in the 1930s after the Hawthorne studies led by Elton Mayo. Researchers found that workers’ productivity increased when they felt observed and valued, highlighting the importance of social factors, morale, and group dynamics.

\term{Motivation‑Hygiene theory} (Herzberg) and \term{Theory X/Y} (McGregor) are later extensions that focus on employee needs and managerial assumptions about worker behaviour.

\begin{example}
A supervisor holds weekly informal meetings where employees can voice concerns. Attendance improves and output rises, illustrating Mayo’s finding that attention to workers’ feelings boosts performance.
\end{example}

\subsection{Quantitative (operations research) approach}

During World War II, managers used mathematical models to allocate scarce resources. After the war, this quantitative approach spread to business. Techniques such as linear programming, simulation, and decision analysis helped managers optimise production, inventory, and scheduling.

\begin{example}
A company uses linear programming to minimise the cost of producing two products while meeting demand and resource constraints. The model identifies the optimal mix of products and the required labor hours.
\end{example}

\subsection{Systems theory}

Systems theory views an organisation as an open system that interacts with its environment. It stresses interdependence of subsystems, feedback loops, and the need for alignment between inputs, processes, and outputs.

\term{Open system} means the organisation exchanges material, information, and energy with its external environment.

\begin{example}
A retail chain monitors supplier performance, customer satisfaction, and market trends. Changes in any of these external factors trigger adjustments in inventory policies and staffing levels.
\end{example}

\subsection{Contingency theory}

Contingency theory rejects a one‑size‑fits‑all approach. It argues that the best management style depends on situational variables such as technology, size, and environment. Researchers like Lawrence and Lorsch identified differentiation and integration as key contingency factors.

\begin{example}
A start‑up with a flat structure adopts informal communication because its product is highly innovative. As the firm grows and the market stabilises, it adds formal hierarchies and standard operating procedures.
\end{example}

\subsection{Modern perspectives}

Recent schools include \term{Total Quality Management} (TQM), \term{Lean}, and \term{Agile}. TQM emphasises continuous improvement and customer focus. Lean removes waste from processes. Agile promotes iterative development and rapid response to change, especially in software projects.

\begin{example}
A software team uses Scrum (an Agile framework). They work in two‑week sprints, hold daily stand‑up meetings, and deliver a potentially shippable product increment at the end of each sprint.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Identify a real organisation you know. List which historical management school best explains its current structure and why, citing at least two specific practices.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Match each management thinker (Taylor, Fayol, Weber, Mayo, Herzberg, McGregor) with the correct school of thought.
  \item Explain how the Hawthorne studies changed the view of worker motivation.
  \item A company wants to reduce production costs using linear programming. List the three main steps in building the model.
  \item Discuss how contingency theory would advise a firm facing rapid technological change.
  \item Choose one modern perspective (TQM, Lean, Agile) and describe a concrete practice that illustrates its principle.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Scientific management & Management approach that uses scientific methods to determine the most efficient way to perform a task. \\
Administrative management & Theory that defines management functions and principles for organisational effectiveness. \\
Bureaucratic management & Model that stresses formal hierarchy, rules, and impersonal relationships to achieve efficiency. \\
Human relations movement & School that highlights the importance of social factors, employee morale, and group dynamics. \\
Quantitative approach & Use of mathematical and statistical techniques to support decision making and optimise operations. \\
Systems theory & Perspective that treats an organisation as an open system interacting with its environment. \\
Contingency theory & Idea that the best management practice depends on situational variables. \\
Total Quality Management (TQM) & Management philosophy focused on continuous improvement and customer satisfaction. \\
Lean & Approach that seeks to eliminate waste and improve flow in processes. \\
Agile & Framework that promotes iterative development, flexibility, and rapid response to change. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{3}
\section{Planning and Decision‑Making}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define what planning means in a project context;
  \item list the main steps of a decision‑making process;
  \item apply a simple decision matrix to choose between alternatives;
  \item recognise common pitfalls in planning and decision‑making.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{What is planning?}

\term{Planning} is the activity of setting goals, identifying tasks, estimating resources, and arranging them in a logical order before work begins. A plan shows *what* will be done, *when*, and *by whom*. For example, a student project might have a plan that lists literature review, prototype design, testing, and report writing, each with a start and finish date.

\begin{example}
A group of three students creates a Gantt‑style table. Task 1 (literature review) runs weeks 1‑2, Task 2 (design) weeks 3‑4, Task 3 (implementation) weeks 5‑7, and Task 4 (report) weeks 8‑9. The table makes clear who is responsible for each task and when it should be finished.
\end{example}

\subsection{Decision‑making process}

A systematic decision‑making process usually follows these steps:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \term{Identify the problem.} State clearly what needs to be decided.
  \item \term{Gather information.} Collect relevant data, constraints, and preferences.
  \item \term{Generate alternatives.} List all feasible options.
  \item \term{Evaluate alternatives.} Use criteria such as cost, time, risk, and quality.
  \item \term{Choose an alternative.} Select the option with the best overall score.
  \item \term{Implement and review.} Put the decision into action and check the outcome.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
A student must choose a programming language for a final project. The problem is “Which language will let me finish on time?” Information gathered includes personal familiarity, library support, and project requirements. Alternatives are Python, Java, and C++. Criteria are learning curve (1‑5), library availability (1‑5), and execution speed (1‑5). After scoring, Python scores highest and is chosen.
\end{example}

\subsection{Decision matrix}

A \term{decision matrix} is a table that assigns numeric scores to each alternative for each criterion, then sums the weighted scores.

\[
\text{Score}_i = \sum_{j=1}^{n} w_j \times s_{ij}
\]

where \(w_j\) is the weight of criterion \(j\) (total weight = 1) and \(s_{ij}\) is the score of alternative \(i\) on criterion \(j\).

\begin{example}
Three tools (A, B, C) are evaluated on cost (weight 0.5) and ease of use (weight 0.5). Scores (out of 10) are:
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
Tool & Cost & Ease \\
A & 8 & 6 \\
B & 5 & 9 \\
C & 7 & 7 \\
\end{tabular}
Overall scores: A = 0.5·8 + 0.5·6 = 7, B = 0.5·5 + 0.5·9 = 7, C = 0.5·7 + 0.5·7 = 7. All tie, so a secondary criterion (e.g., availability) can break the tie.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} For a given choice, list the problem, at least two criteria, and compute a simple decision matrix. If the matrix gives a clear highest score, you have applied the method correctly.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Common pitfalls}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Over‑optimistic scheduling.} Assuming tasks will finish faster than realistic.
  \item \term{Ignoring constraints.} Forgetting budget limits or resource availability.
  \item \term{Bias in criteria weighting.} Giving too much weight to a favourite option.
  \item \term{Insufficient alternatives.} Not considering enough possible solutions.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A project plan lists all tasks as one‑day activities, ignoring that testing usually takes longer. The schedule collapses when testing overruns, causing missed deadlines.
\end{example}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Define planning in your own words and give a real‑world example.
  \item Outline the six steps of the decision‑making process for choosing a study location.
  \item Create a decision matrix for selecting a laptop, using criteria of price, battery life, and performance. Assign weights that sum to 1 and compute the scores.
  \item Identify two pitfalls that could affect the plan you created in Exercise 1 and suggest how to avoid them.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Planning & Setting goals, tasks, resources, and timelines before work starts. \\
Decision‑making & A structured process to choose among alternatives based on criteria. \\
Decision matrix & Tabular tool that scores alternatives against weighted criteria. \\
Criterion & A factor used to judge alternatives (e.g., cost, risk). \\
Pitfall & A common mistake that can undermine a plan or decision. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{4}
\section{Organizing and Structure}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item describe the purpose of logical organization in a database;
  \item differentiate between tables, records, and fields;
  \item explain primary keys and foreign keys;
  \item outline a simple hierarchical structure for related data.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Logical organization}

A \term{logical organization} is the way data is grouped so that related items are stored together and can be accessed efficiently. In a relational DBMS this means arranging data into \term{tables}. Each table represents one entity type, such as \texttt{Students} or \texttt{Courses}. Logical organization does not depend on how the data is physically stored on disk; it is defined by the schema.

\begin{example}
A university database has three tables: \texttt{Students}, \texttt{Courses}, and \texttt{Enrollments}. The \texttt{Students} table holds personal details, the \texttt{Courses} table holds course titles, and the \texttt{Enrollments} table links students to the courses they take. This logical split makes it easy to add a new course without changing the student records.
\end{example}

\subsection{Tables, records, and fields}

A \term{table} is a two‑dimensional collection of data. The vertical parts are called \term{fields} (or columns) and store a single attribute, such as \texttt{StudentID} or \texttt{Email}. The horizontal parts are called \term{records} (or rows) and store a complete set of attributes for one entity.

\begin{example}
In the \texttt{Students} table, the fields might be \texttt{StudentID}, \texttt{Name}, \texttt{DateOfBirth}, and \texttt{Email}. One record could be \texttt{(2023001, "Ada Lee", "2002-04-15", "ada.lee@example.com")}.
\end{example}

\subsection{Primary keys}

A \term{primary key} is a field (or a combination of fields) that uniquely identifies each record in a table. No two records can have the same primary key value, and a primary key cannot be null. This ensures that each entity can be referenced unambiguously.

\begin{example}
In the \texttt{Students} table, \texttt{StudentID} is chosen as the primary key because each student receives a unique number at enrolment.
\end{example}

\subsection{Foreign keys and relationships}

A \term{foreign key} is a field in one table that refers to the primary key of another table. Foreign keys create \term{relationships} between tables, allowing data to be linked without duplication.

\begin{example}
The \texttt{Enrollments} table has two foreign keys: \texttt{StudentID} (referencing \texttt{Students.StudentID}) and \texttt{CourseID} (referencing \texttt{Courses.CourseID}). This records which student is enrolled in which course.
\end{example}

\subsection{Hierarchical structure}

When data naturally forms levels, a hierarchical structure can be used. The top level contains a parent entity, and each lower level contains child entities linked by foreign keys. This pattern is common in organisational charts, product categories, and file systems.

\begin{example}
A company database might have a \texttt{Departments} table (parent) and an \texttt{Employees} table (child). Each employee record includes a \texttt{DeptID} foreign key that points to the department they belong to.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Identify a table in your own project. State its primary key and at least one foreign key that links it to another table.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item List the fields you would include in a \texttt{Books} table for a library system. Indicate which field should be the primary key.
  \item Given tables \texttt{Authors} and \texttt{Books}, propose a foreign key to represent the author of each book.
  \item Draw a simple hierarchy for a school: include tables for \texttt{Schools}, \texttt{Classes}, and \texttt{Students}, and specify the foreign keys.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Logical organization & Grouping data into tables based on entity types. \\
Table & A collection of records with the same fields. \\
Record & One row in a table, representing a single entity. \\
Field & One column in a table, representing an attribute. \\
Primary key & A unique, non‑null identifier for each record in a table. \\
Foreign key & A field that references the primary key of another table, creating a relationship. \\
Hierarchy & A multi‑level structure where child tables link to parent tables via foreign keys. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{5}
\section{Leadership Styles and Motivation}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item describe the main \term{leadership styles};
  \item explain how each style influences employee \term{motivation};
  \item apply a simple model to match a style with a motivational need;
  \item evaluate the suitability of a style for a given team situation.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Leadership styles}

\term{Autocratic leadership} is a style in which the leader makes decisions alone and expects compliance. Communication is top‑down and rules are strict.

\term{Democratic leadership} involves the leader sharing decision‑making with the group. Team members discuss options and the leader facilitates consensus.

\term{Laissez‑faire leadership} gives the team high autonomy. The leader provides resources but intervenes little in day‑to‑day work.

\begin{example}
A project manager decides the software architecture without consulting developers (autocratic). In another sprint, the same manager asks the team to vote on the user‑interface design (democratic). For routine maintenance tasks, the manager lets the senior engineers choose their own methods (laissez‑faire).
\end{example}

\subsection{Motivation basics}

\term{Intrinsic motivation} comes from within the individual – interest, enjoyment, personal growth.  

\term{Extrinsic motivation} is driven by external rewards – pay, recognition, promotions.

\term{Maslow’s hierarchy of needs} suggests people are motivated first by basic physiological needs, then safety, belonging, esteem, and finally self‑actualisation.

\begin{example}
A programmer who loves solving puzzles works late because the challenge is rewarding (intrinsic). The same programmer may also stay late to meet a deadline that offers a bonus (extrinsic).
\end{example}

\subsection{Linking styles to motivation}

Autocratic leaders often rely on extrinsic motivators such as bonuses or penalties. This can be effective when tasks are routine and safety is critical.

Democratic leaders tend to foster intrinsic motivation by giving employees a voice, increasing their sense of belonging and esteem.

Laissez‑faire leaders support self‑actualisation by allowing experts to pursue innovative solutions, but may fail if basic needs for direction and security are unmet.

\begin{example}
In a call‑centre, an autocratic supervisor uses performance targets and strict scripts to ensure consistency (extrinsic). In a research lab, a democratic PI holds weekly brainstorming meetings, encouraging researchers to pursue ideas they find personally meaningful (intrinsic). In a software start‑up, the CTO adopts a laissez‑faire approach, trusting senior developers to design architecture, which boosts their sense of mastery (self‑actualisation).
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Choose a team situation. Identify the dominant need from Maslow’s hierarchy. Then select the leadership style that most directly satisfies that need. Explain why the chosen style is appropriate.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Match each leadership style with the type of motivation (intrinsic or extrinsic) it most commonly uses. Explain your reasoning.
  \item A team is struggling with unclear goals and low morale. Which style would you adopt first, and which motivational need does it address?
  \item Describe a scenario where a laissez‑faire style could reduce motivation. Suggest a minimal intervention to improve motivation.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Autocratic leadership & Leader makes decisions alone; expects obedience. \\
Democratic leadership & Leader shares decision‑making; encourages participation. \\
Laissez‑faire leadership & Leader provides autonomy; minimal direct control. \\
Intrinsic motivation & Motivation driven by internal satisfaction or interest. \\
Extrinsic motivation & Motivation driven by external rewards or pressures. \\
Maslow’s hierarchy of needs & A five‑level model of human needs from basic to self‑actualisation. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{6}
\section{Communication in Organizations}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define \term{organizational communication};
  \item distinguish between formal and informal communication channels;
  \item identify common barriers to effective communication;
  \item explain the role of digital media in organisational communication;
  \item apply basic principles to improve a simple communication process.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Organizational communication}

\term{Organizational communication} is the exchange of information, ideas, and feelings among members of a workplace or institution. It enables coordination of tasks, decision‑making, and the development of a shared culture.

\begin{example}
In a small company, a manager sends a memo to the sales team outlining a new pricing policy. The memo conveys the policy (information) and invites the team to ask questions (feedback), thereby coordinating future sales activities.
\end{example}

\subsection{Formal and informal channels}

\term{Formal channels} are official pathways defined by the organisation’s structure, such as reports, meetings, and official emails.  
\term{Informal channels} arise spontaneously, such as water‑cooler chats, instant messaging groups, or social media interactions.

\begin{example}
A project manager holds a weekly status meeting (formal) but also creates a group chat on a messaging app where team members share quick updates and troubleshoot problems (informal). Both channels together keep the project on track.
\end{example}

\subsection{Barriers to effective communication}

Common barriers include:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \term{Noise} – physical or psychological distractions that distort the message.
  \item \term{Semantic differences} – use of jargon or ambiguous terms.
  \item \term{Hierarchical distance} – reluctance to speak up to senior staff.
  \item \term{Cultural differences} – varying norms and expectations.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
An engineer uses technical abbreviations in an email to the marketing department. The marketers misinterpret the abbreviations, leading to a product description error. The barrier here is semantic difference.
\end{example}

\subsection{Digital media in organisational communication}

Digital tools (email, intranet, collaboration platforms) increase speed and reach but require clear protocols. Effective digital communication follows:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Clarity:} concise subject lines and plain language.
  \item \textbf{Appropriate medium:} choose email for formal records, instant messaging for quick questions.
  \item \textbf{Documentation:} archive important decisions in a shared repository.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A department adopts a project‑management platform where tasks are assigned, progress is logged, and comments are time‑stamped. This reduces reliance on ad‑hoc emails and ensures a transparent record of work.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Identify a recent communication exchange in your workplace. Classify each message as formal or informal, note any barrier that appeared, and suggest one digital tool that could improve the process.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Write a short definition of \term{organizational communication} in your own words.
  \item List three formal and three informal communication channels you use in a typical university setting.
  \item Choose one barrier from the list above and describe a concrete step to overcome it in a team project.
  \item Draft an email subject line and first sentence that follow the clarity guidelines for a meeting reminder.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Organizational communication & Exchange of information within a workplace to coordinate activities and build culture. \\
Formal channel & Official, structured means of communication defined by the organisation’s hierarchy. \\
Informal channel & Spontaneous, unofficial means of communication that arise among members. \\
Noise & Any disturbance that interferes with the accurate transmission of a message. \\
Semantic difference & Misunderstanding caused by ambiguous or specialised language. \\
Digital media & Electronic tools such as email, intranets, and collaboration platforms used for communication. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{7}
\section{Managing Teams and Groups}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define what a team and a group are and explain the difference;
  \item describe the five stages of team development;
  \item identify common team roles and their purposes;
  \item apply basic techniques for effective communication and conflict resolution;
  \item choose an appropriate leadership style for a given team situation.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Team vs. group}

A \term{group} is a collection of individuals who share a common context but do not necessarily work together toward a single goal. For example, all students in a lecture hall form a group.

A \term{team} is a subset of a group that has a clear, shared objective, interdependent tasks, and mutual accountability. A project team of four students writing a report is a team.

\begin{example}
In a university, the entire class of 120 students is a group. The four students assigned to design the lab experiment form a team because they must coordinate their work to produce a single report.
\end{example}

\subsection{Stages of team development}

Bruce Tuckman identified five stages that most teams experience:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \term{Forming.} Members meet, learn the task, and are polite.
  \item \term{Storming.} Differences emerge; conflict may arise over ideas or roles.
  \item \term{Norming.} The team establishes norms, roles become clear, and cooperation improves.
  \item \term{Performing.} The team works efficiently toward the goal with minimal friction.
  \item \term{Adjourning.} The task is completed and the team disbands.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
A semester‑long software project starts with a kickoff meeting (forming). Mid‑semester, two members argue about coding standards (storming). They agree on a shared style guide (norming). In the final weeks they deliver the product on time (performing). After the demo, the team members return to their regular courses (adjourning).
\end{example}

\subsection{Common team roles}

Teams often contain a mix of the following roles:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Leader/Coordinator} – sets direction, facilitates meetings.
  \item \term{Recorder/Documenter} – captures decisions and produces reports.
  \item \term{Implementer/Doer} – carries out the technical work.
  \item \term{Checker/Reviewer} – checks quality and ensures standards.
  \item \term{Facilitator/Communicator} – keeps information flowing and resolves misunderstandings.
\end{itemize}

Roles are not fixed; members may rotate to develop skills.

\begin{example}
In a marketing team, Alice acts as leader, Bob records meeting minutes, Carla creates the campaign graphics (implementer), Dan reviews the copy for errors (checker), and Eva ensures everyone hears each other’s ideas (facilitator).
\end{example}

\subsection{Effective communication}

Clear communication reduces errors and builds trust. Use these practices:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Active listening} – repeat back key points to confirm understanding.
  \item \term{Structured updates} – use a brief agenda: what was done, what is planned, what blocks exist.
  \item \term{Written records} – follow up verbal discussions with concise notes.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
During a weekly stand‑up, each member states: “Yesterday I coded the login page, today I will test it, and I need the API key from the backend team.” The leader writes these points in a shared document for reference.
\end{example}

\subsection{Conflict resolution}

Conflict is natural; handle it with a simple three‑step process:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \term{Identify the issue.} State the problem without blame.
  \item \term{Explore perspectives.} Let each side explain their view.
  \item \term{Agree on action.} Decide a concrete step that satisfies both parties.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
Two members disagree on the colour palette. They first state the disagreement (“We cannot agree on the colour”). Each explains their reasoning (brand consistency vs. visual appeal). They decide to test both palettes with a sample audience and choose the one with higher preference.
\end{example}

\subsection{Choosing a leadership style}

Four common styles match different team needs:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Directive.} Leader gives clear instructions; useful in early forming or crisis.
  \item \term{Supportive.} Leader offers encouragement; helps during storming.
  \item \term{Participative.} Leader involves the team in decisions; fits norming and performing.
  \item \term{Delegative.} Leader trusts the team to act autonomously; works well in mature performing teams.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A new research group starts with a professor giving detailed tasks (directive). As the group gains confidence, the professor shifts to participative meetings where members propose experiment designs.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} You are assigned to lead a five‑person project that has just begun. Which leadership style should you adopt first, and why?
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Distinguish a group from a team using an example from your campus life.
  \item List the five stages of team development and give a brief sign that a team is in each stage.
  \item Match each of the five common roles to a task you have performed in a recent group assignment.
  \item Role‑play a conflict using the three‑step resolution process; write down the agreed action.
  \item Choose a leadership style for a team that is already performing well and justify your choice.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Group & A collection of individuals sharing a context but not necessarily a common goal. \\
Team & A group with a shared objective, interdependent tasks, and mutual accountability. \\
Forming & Initial stage where members get to know each other and the task. \\
Storming & Stage where conflicts and differences surface. \\
Norming & Stage where norms and roles are established and cooperation improves. \\
Performing & Stage of efficient, goal‑oriented work with little friction. \\
Adjourning & Stage where the team disbands after completing its task. \\
Active listening & Technique of restating speaker’s points to confirm understanding. \\
Directive leadership & Style where the leader provides clear, specific instructions. \\
Participative leadership & Style where the leader involves the team in decision‑making. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{8}
\section{Controlling and Performance Measurement}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define \term{controlling} in a management context;
  \item describe the steps of the controlling process;
  \item explain why performance measurement is needed;
  \item apply simple performance measures to a small business example;
  \item distinguish between financial and non‑financial performance indicators.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Controlling}

\term{Controlling} is the management function that monitors actual performance, compares it with planned targets, and takes corrective action when necessary. It closes the loop between planning and execution.

The controlling process consists of four steps:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Set standards.} Define quantitative or qualitative targets (e.g., sales of \$10\,000 per month).
  \item \textbf{Measure actual performance.} Collect data on the relevant variables.
  \item \textbf{Compare actual with standards.} Calculate variances.
  \item \textbf{Take corrective action.} Adjust resources, processes or goals.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
A bakery plans to sell 500 loaves per week. At the end of the week it sold 420 loaves. The variance is -80 loaves. The manager decides to increase advertising and to adjust the baking schedule to meet the target next week.
\end{example}

\subsection{Performance Measurement}

\term{Performance measurement} is the systematic collection and analysis of data that indicates how well an organization or unit is achieving its objectives. Measures can be \term{financial} (e.g., profit margin) or \term{non‑financial} (e.g., customer satisfaction).

Common categories of performance measures:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Financial measures.} Revenue, cost, return on investment.
  \item \textbf{Operational measures.} Production volume, cycle time, defect rate.
  \item \textbf{Strategic measures.} Market share, brand awareness.
  \item \textbf{Human resource measures.} Employee turnover, training hours.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A small electronics shop tracks two indicators each month: (1) total sales revenue (financial) and (2) average customer rating on a 5‑point scale (non‑financial). In March the revenue was \$12\,000 and the rating was 4.2; in April revenue rose to \$13\,500 but the rating fell to 3.9, signalling a need to improve service quality.
\end{example}

\subsection{Key test}
\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Identify a process in your daily life, write down a target for it, measure the actual result, compute the variance, and suggest one corrective action.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item List the four steps of the controlling process and give a brief example for each.
  \item Differentiate between a financial and a non‑financial performance measure with an example from a retail store.
  \item A factory sets a target of 1\,000 units per day. It produces 950 units on Monday and 1\,050 units on Tuesday. Calculate the daily variances and suggest a possible reason for the difference.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Controlling & Management function that monitors performance, compares with targets, and initiates corrective action. \\
Performance measurement & Systematic collection and analysis of data to assess achievement of objectives. \\
Financial measure & Indicator expressed in monetary terms, such as profit or revenue. \\
Non‑financial measure & Indicator not expressed in money, such as quality or satisfaction. \\
Variance & Difference between actual performance and the planned standard. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{9}
\section{Change Management and Innovation}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define \term{change management} and explain its purpose;
  \item describe the main stages of a change management process;
  \item distinguish between incremental and radical \term{innovation};
  \item apply a simple model to assess readiness for change;
  \item identify common barriers to successful change and propose ways to overcome them.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{What is change management?}

\term{Change management} is a structured approach that prepares, supports, and helps individuals, teams, and organisations transition from a current state to a desired future state. It focuses on the human side of change, ensuring that people understand why change is needed, what will happen, and how they will be supported.

\begin{example}
A university introduces a new online learning platform. The IT department plans training sessions, creates FAQs, and appoints “change champions” among faculty to help colleagues adapt. This systematic effort is change management.
\end{example}

\subsection{Stages of a change management process}

A common model uses four stages:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \term{Preparation.} Identify the need for change, define objectives, and secure leadership support.
  \item \term{Planning.} Develop a detailed plan that includes timelines, resources, communication strategies, and risk assessment.
  \item \term{Implementation.} Execute the plan, provide training, and monitor progress.
  \item \term{Sustainment.} Reinforce new behaviours, collect feedback, and make adjustments to embed the change.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
A college wants to replace paper grade books with a digital grading system. In the preparation stage, the dean explains the benefits. In planning, a project team creates a rollout schedule. During implementation, staff attend workshops and pilot the system. In sustainment, the college reviews usage data and offers ongoing support.
\end{example}

\subsection{Innovation: incremental vs. radical}

\term{Innovation} is the introduction of new ideas, products, or processes that add value. It can be:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Incremental innovation}: small, continuous improvements to existing products or processes (e.g., adding a new feature to an app).
  \item \term{Radical innovation}: breakthrough changes that create new markets or fundamentally alter how things are done (e.g., the shift from printed textbooks to interactive e‑learning platforms).
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A library adds a mobile app for reserving books – incremental. The same library later adopts a blockchain system for secure digital credentialing – radical.
\end{example}

\subsection{Assessing readiness for change}

The \term{readiness assessment} helps decide whether an organisation is prepared for change. A simple checklist includes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Leadership commitment;
  \item Clear vision and goals;
  \item Availability of resources;
  \item Staff skills and attitudes;
  \item Existing communication channels.
\end{itemize}

If most items are positive, the organisation is ready; otherwise, the plan should address gaps before proceeding.

\begin{example}
Before launching a new student portal, the project team surveys faculty. Results show strong leadership support but limited technical skills. The team adds a training module to the plan.
\end{example}

\subsection{Barriers to successful change and how to overcome them}

Common barriers:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \term{Resistance due to fear of loss}. People worry about job security or competence.
  \item \term{Lack of clear communication}. Uncertainty breeds rumors.
  \item \term{Insufficient resources}. Without time or budget, change stalls.
  \item \term{Cultural inertia}. Established habits are hard to break.
\end{enumerate}

Ways to overcome:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Involve staff early and address concerns openly.
  \item Provide regular, transparent updates.
  \item Allocate realistic budgets and protect time for training.
  \item Celebrate quick wins to build momentum.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
During a curriculum redesign, the department holds focus groups, publishes a timeline, secures a modest training fund, and publicly recognises teams that adopt new teaching methods.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Choose a recent change in your institution (e.g., new software, policy, or teaching method). Identify the stage of the change management process it is in, the type of innovation involved, and at least two barriers that have been addressed or remain.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Define change management in your own words and give a non‑academic example.
  \item List the four stages of a change management process and match each with an activity you might perform in a university setting.
  \item Differentiate incremental and radical innovation with examples from education technology.
  \item Conduct a brief readiness assessment for a proposed change (e.g., introducing a new grading system) using the checklist provided.
  \item Identify three common barriers to change and propose one specific action to mitigate each.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Change management & Structured approach to help people move from a current to a desired state. \\
Preparation & First stage: recognise need for change and secure leadership. \\
Planning & Second stage: create detailed roadmap, resources, and communication plan. \\
Implementation & Third stage: execute the plan, train users, monitor progress. \\
Sustainment & Fourth stage: embed change, collect feedback, adjust as needed. \\
Innovation & Introduction of new ideas, products, or processes that add value. \\
Incremental innovation & Small, continuous improvements to existing offerings. \\
Radical innovation & Breakthrough changes that create new markets or ways of working. \\
Readiness assessment & Checklist to gauge an organisation’s preparedness for change. \\
Resistance & Opposition or fear that hampers adoption of change. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{10}
\section{Ethics, Social Responsibility, and Global Management}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define \term{business ethics} and give a simple example;
  \item explain the concept of \term{corporate social responsibility} (CSR) and list two common CSR activities;
  \item describe the main challenges of managing organisations across national borders;
  \item apply an ethical decision‑making framework to a short case.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Business ethics}

\term{Business ethics} are the moral principles that guide the behaviour of individuals and organisations in commercial activities. They address questions such as: Is it fair to pay workers a living wage? Should a company hide negative test results of a product?

\begin{example}
A small electronics firm discovers that a batch of batteries overheats. Management decides whether to recall the product (incurring costs) or to wait for customer complaints. An ethical choice would be to issue a recall immediately to protect users.
\end{example}

\subsection{Corporate social responsibility (CSR)}

\term{Corporate social responsibility} (CSR) is the idea that companies should contribute positively to society beyond profit making. CSR can involve environmental protection, community development, fair labour practices, and ethical sourcing.

Two common CSR activities:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Reducing carbon emissions by adopting renewable energy.
  \item Supporting local education through scholarships or school building projects.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A clothing retailer audits its supply chain and discovers a factory where workers are paid below the minimum wage. The retailer decides to work with the factory to raise wages and improve working conditions, publishing the results in an annual CSR report.
\end{example}

\subsection{Global management challenges}

Managing a multinational organisation introduces cultural, legal, and operational difficulties.

Key challenges:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \term{Cultural differences}. Norms about hierarchy, communication, and time vary across countries.
  \item \term{Legal and regulatory variation}. Labour laws, tax regimes, and environmental standards differ.
  \item \term{Coordination across distance}. Time‑zone differences and remote teams complicate decision‑making.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
A US‑based software company opens a development centre in India. The US team prefers direct, quick feedback, while the Indian team values consensus and detailed documentation. Misunderstandings arise until the manager establishes a shared protocol that respects both styles.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} When evaluating a business decision, ask: (1) Does the action respect basic moral principles such as honesty and fairness? (2) Does it create value for stakeholders beyond shareholders? (3) Have I considered cultural and legal differences that may affect implementation in other countries?
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Identify a recent news story involving a company’s ethical dilemma. State the dilemma and the ethical principle involved.
  \item List three CSR activities a fast‑food chain could undertake and explain how each benefits both society and the company.
  \item A multinational firm faces a conflict between a home‑country law and a host‑country regulation. Propose a step‑by‑step approach to resolve the conflict.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Business ethics & Moral principles that guide conduct in commercial activities. \\
Corporate social responsibility (CSR) & Voluntary actions by a company to benefit society beyond profit. \\
Cultural differences & Variations in values, norms, and behaviours among societies. \\
Legal and regulatory variation & Different laws and rules that apply in each country. \\
Stakeholders & Individuals or groups affected by or affecting an organisation’s actions. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
